{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-user `max_feeds` limit (0 = unlimited). The build and existing unit and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself has not been exercised by any test here.\n\n- **Schema and user object**: new migration adds `users.max_feeds int not null default 0`; the field is on the user model, the modification request, all user queries, and the Go client's `User` / `UserModificationRequest`.\n- **Admin only**: `PUT /v1/users/{userID}` returns 403 when a non-admin sends a `max_feeds` different from the current value; negative values return 400. A non-admin sending the unchanged value is accepted.\n- **Enforcement**: `storage.CreateFeed` is the only place feeds are inserted. It now locks the user row, counts feeds and inserts in one transaction, returning `ErrFeedLimitReached` at the limit, so concurrent requests cannot exceed it together. This covers the API, UI, Google Reader and OPML paths.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`**: returns 400 and creates nothing when the limit is reached; the check also runs before the feed is fetched.\n- **`POST /v1/import`**: stops at the limit and returns 400. Feeds imported before the limit was hit are kept, so an oversized import is partial rather than rejected outright.\n\nThings you might not expect:\n- The limit-reached and negative-value messages are plain English strings, not translation keys; I did not add entries to the locale files.\n- The Google Reader and UI paths block creation, but I did not give them a dedicated status or message.\n- Lowering `max_feeds` below a user's current feed count is allowed; it only blocks new feeds.\n- I updated the `@effects` tags on the functions I changed directly (`CreateFeed`, OPML `Import`, the import handler); tags on indirect callers and `graph.json` were not regenerated.", "t": 1794461960.903}
